          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which combination of findings best identifies posthepatic (obstructive) jaundice</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which combination of findings best identifies posthepatic (obstructive) jaundice?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Raised unconjugated bilirubin, negative urine bilirubin, increased urobilinogen</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This pattern indicates prehepatic jaundice, as seen in haemolysis. Unconjugated bilirubin is not water-soluble and cannot be filtered by the kidneys, so urine bilirubin remains negative.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Markedly raised conjugated bilirubin, elevated alkaline phosphatase and GGT, pale stools</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In posthepatic obstruction, conjugated bilirubin backs up into blood and appears in urine, while absent stercobilin makes stools pale. ALP and GGT rise because bile duct epithelial cells release them under cholestatic pressure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Elevated transaminases with mixed hyperbilirubinaemia and impaired synthetic markers</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This pattern points to hepatic jaundice caused by hepatocyte injury. The mixed bilirubin elevation reflects both impaired conjugation and leakage of conjugated bilirubin from damaged cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Normal liver enzymes with isolated unconjugated hyperbilirubinaemia after fasting</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This presentation suggests Gilbert syndrome, a mild UGT1A1 deficiency. It is benign and does not involve biliary obstruction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In posthepatic jaundice, bile cannot reach the gut, so conjugated bilirubin backs up into blood and urine, stools become pale from absent stercobilin, and ALP/GGT rise from cholestatic pressure on bile duct epithelium."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Posthepatic jaundice shows a cholestatic pattern — markedly raised conjugated bilirubin, ALP, and GGT — because bile flow is blocked downstream of conjugation, preventing bilirubin from reaching the gut."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
