        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 347.9157083333333,
            "to": 357.9157083333333
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What is the primary mechanism of hepatocyte injury in hepatitis B infection?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What is the primary mechanism of hepatocyte injury in hepatitis B infection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Direct viral lysis of infected hepatocytes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>HBV is not directly cytopathic. The virus itself does not lyse hepatocytes; the clinical outcome depends entirely on the strength of the host immune response.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CD8-positive cytotoxic T lymphocytes recognising viral peptides on MHC class I molecules</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Infected hepatocytes display viral peptides on MHC class I, prompting CTL-mediated killing. A vigorous response clears infection, a weak neonatal response allows chronicity, and a massive response causes fulminant failure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Complement-mediated destruction triggered by circulating immune complexes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Immune complexes may cause extrahepatic manifestations such as polyarteritis nodosa, but the primary hepatocyte injury is T-cell mediated, not complement-driven.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reactive metabolite formation depleting glutathione stores</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Glutathione depletion by NAPQI is the mechanism in paracetamol overdose, where CYP2E1 converts excess drug into the reactive metabolite. This does not occur in viral hepatitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "HBV is not directly cytopathic. Hepatocyte destruction is driven by CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes that recognise viral peptides displayed on MHC class I molecules on infected cells."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! HBV injury is immune-mediated — the strength of the CD8+ CTL response determines whether infection is cleared, becomes chronic, or causes fulminant failure."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
