          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which aetiological factor causes hepatocellular carcinoma through direct oncogen</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which aetiological factor causes hepatocellular carcinoma through direct oncogenesis without requiring pre-existing cirrhosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatitis C virus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>HCV causes hepatocellular carcinoma exclusively through the cirrhosis pathway. Unlike HBV, it does not integrate into the host genome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatitis B virus</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. HBV integrates viral DNA into hepatocyte nuclei via the HBx protein, enabling direct oncogenesis even before cirrhosis develops. This is why HCC can occur in young HBV carriers without established cirrhosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Aflatoxin B1</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Aflatoxin B1 causes a specific TP53 mutation at codon 249, but it typically acts as a co-carcinogen alongside chronic viral hepatitis rather than as an independent driver of direct oncogenesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Non-alcoholic steatohepatitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>NASH contributes to hepatocellular carcinoma primarily through progression to cirrhosis, not through direct oncogenic mechanisms.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "HBV integrates its DNA into hepatocyte nuclei via the HBx protein, enabling direct oncogenesis even before cirrhosis develops. HCV, in contrast, causes HCC only through the cirrhosis pathway."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! HBV is unique among hepatitis viruses in its ability to cause HCC through direct oncogenesis via the HBx protein, even in the absence of cirrhosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
