        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 931.6195,
            "to": 941.6195
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A liver biopsy shows regenerative nodules completely surrounded by fibrous septa</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A liver biopsy shows regenerative nodules completely surrounded by fibrous septa, with no identifiable central veins. Which condition does this histological pattern indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatic steatosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Steatosis shows clear fat vacuoles pushing the nucleus to the periphery (signet-ring appearance), but the normal lobular architecture including central veins is preserved.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cirrhosis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Loss of normal architecture with regenerative nodules encircled by fibrous septa and absent central veins is the hallmark of cirrhosis. Architecture is the first and most important discriminator at the bench.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Alcoholic hepatitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Alcoholic hepatitis shows ballooning degeneration, Mallory-Denk bodies (eosinophilic cytokeratin inclusions), and neutrophilic satellitosis, but lobular architecture is still preserved at this stage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acute viral hepatitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Acute viral hepatitis shows ballooning degeneration, Councilman bodies, and lobular lymphocytic disarray, but the overall lobular architecture remains intact.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key finding is complete loss of normal architecture. Regenerative nodules enclosed by fibrous septa without identifiable central veins defines cirrhosis — always assess architecture first when reading a liver slide."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! Architecture is your first discriminator — regenerative nodules surrounded by fibrous septa with loss of central veins confirms cirrhosis, distinguishing it from conditions that preserve lobular architecture."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
